      "question": {
        ":question/id": "969e4ff4-2cc5-505c-bc08-46e0ae40a225",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.$$\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}x&=3\\cos\\theta\\\\x^2&=(3\\cos\\theta)^2\\\\&=9\\cos^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}y&=3\\sin\\theta\\\\y^2&=(3\\sin\\theta)^2\\\\&=9\\sin^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using the identity, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=9\\cos^2\\theta+9\\sin^2\\theta\\\\&=9(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\\\&=9(1)\\\\&=9.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^{2}+y^{2}=9.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
