      "question": {
        ":question/id": "eaf5e3f7-8e64-5b18-999b-2cd25904a7b4",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{11}{2}\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\frac{11}{2}\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.$$\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{11}{2}\\cos\\theta\\\\x^2&=(\\frac{11}{2}\\cos\\theta)^2\\\\&=\\frac{121}{4}\\cos^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}y&=\\frac{11}{2}\\sin\\theta\\\\y^2&=(\\frac{11}{2}\\sin\\theta)^2\\\\&=\\frac{121}{4}\\sin^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using the identity, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=\\frac{121}{4}\\cos^2\\theta+\\frac{121}{4}\\sin^2\\theta\\\\&=\\frac{121}{4}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\\\&=\\frac{121}{4}(1)\\\\&=\\frac{121}{4}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{121}{4}.$",
