      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "64910b40-f932-55b3-995f-c5192acf4615",
        ":question/problem": "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta+6,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta+\\frac{3}{4},\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x-$ {{field-1}} $)^{2}+(y-$ {{field-2}} $)^{2}=$ {{field-3}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}(x-6)&=3\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{1}{3}(x-6)&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{1}{9}(x-6)^{2}&=\\cos^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}(y-\\frac{3}{4})&=3\\sin\\theta\\\\\\frac{1}{3}(y-\\frac{3}{4})&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\\frac{1}{9}(y-\\frac{3}{4})^{2}&=\\sin^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using the identity, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}(x-6)^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}(y-\\frac{3}{4})^{2}&=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta\\\\&=1\\\\(x-6)^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{4})^{2}&=9.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-6)^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{4})^{2}=9.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
