      "question": {
        ":question/id": "09774461-7acb-5e1c-a2a0-726c7f403174",
        ":question/problem": "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta-4,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta+\\frac{11}{4},\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}(x+4)&=3\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{1}{3}(x+4)&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{1}{9}(x+4)^{2}&=\\cos^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}(y-\\frac{11}{4})&=3\\sin\\theta\\\\\\frac{1}{3}(y-\\frac{11}{4})&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\\frac{1}{9}(y-\\frac{11}{4})^{2}&=\\sin^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using the identity, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}(x+4)^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}(y-\\frac{11}{4})^{2}&=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta\\\\&=1\\\\(x+4)^{2}+(y-\\frac{11}{4})^{2}&=9.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x+4)^{2}+(y-\\frac{11}{4})^{2}=9.$",
