      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "01c5124e-d846-5cc1-ade2-155a3cb67a34",
        ":question/problem": "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{13}{2}\\cos\\theta+5,\\quad y=\\frac{13}{2}\\sin\\theta+\\frac{9}{2},\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x-$ {{field-1}} $)^{2}+(y-$ {{field-2}} $)^{2}=$ {{field-3}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}(x-5)&=\\frac{13}{2}\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{2}{13}(x-5)&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{4}{169}(x-5)^{2}&=\\cos^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}(y-\\frac{9}{2})&=\\frac{13}{2}\\sin\\theta\\\\\\frac{2}{13}(y-\\frac{9}{2})&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\\frac{4}{169}(y-\\frac{9}{2})^{2}&=\\sin^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using the identity, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{169}(x-5)^{2}+\\frac{4}{169}(y-\\frac{9}{2})^{2}&=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta\\\\&=1\\\\(x-5)^{2}+(y-\\frac{9}{2})^{2}&=\\frac{169}{4}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-5)^{2}+(y-\\frac{9}{2})^{2}=\\frac{169}{4}.$",
