      "question": {
        ":question/id": "0a6dea72-671d-5459-8127-b6fc829ccc35",
        ":question/problem": "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=14\\cos\\theta-2,\\quad y=14\\sin\\theta-1,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}(x+2)&=14\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{1}{14}(x+2)&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{1}{196}(x+2)^{2}&=\\cos^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}(y+1)&=14\\sin\\theta\\\\\\frac{1}{14}(y+1)&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\\frac{1}{196}(y+1)^{2}&=\\sin^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using the identity, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{196}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{196}(y+1)^{2}&=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta\\\\&=1\\\\(x+2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}&=196.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x+2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=196.$",
